ble literate prose? Information regarding temporal organization was inevitably lost in the publications. Back channel signals, hesitations, contractions and elisions, and paralinguistic phenomena (e.g., laughter) were virtually eliminated. Turn-taking was regularized, and many discourse markers were removed. Comparisons with another prize-winning interview (Princess Diana with Martin Bashir; BBC, 1995) revealed optional, rather than prescriptive ways of being successful: although spoken syllables in both interviews were allocated to interviewers and interviewees in a 25% to 75% ratio, turns of both interviewer and interviewee were much longer in the Arendt than in the Princess Diana interview; Arendt spoke more slowly and used more hesitations than any other speaker; although both interviewers remained atypically off-camera, their styles were different. Gaus used frequent back-channeling, and Bashir none at all; Bashir articulated more slowly and less hesitantly, interrupted less frequently, and his pauses before taking his turns were twice as long as Gaus's. Characteristics of orality and literacy in excellent public discourse are discussed.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00034-4","pdf":"","k":["Orality","Laughter","German","Interview","Paralanguage","Literacy","Public speaking","Psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00007-1","t":"Agency in foreign news: A linguistic complement of a content analytical study","a":["Dariusz Galasiński","Carol Marley"],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00007-1","pdf":"","k":["Interpretation (philosophy)","Complement (music)","Content analysis","Content (measure theory)","Linguistics","Agency (philosophy)","Linguistic analysis","Quantitative analysis (chemistry)"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00030-7","t":"Whatever — Its use in ‘pseudo-argument’","a":["Brian H. Kleiner"],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00030-7","pdf":"","k":["Argument (complex analysis)","Argumentation theory","Argumentative","Focus (optics)","Bracketing (phenomenology)","Order (exchange)","Epistemology","Rationality"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00029-0","t":"On semantic and pragmatic ambiguity","a":["Elin Fredsted"],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00029-0","pdf":"","k":["Implicature","Ambiguity","Grice","Linguistics","Pragmatics","Computer science","Semantics (computer science)","Semantic theory of truth"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00040-x","t":"Arguments from ignorance","a":["Douglas Walton"],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00040-x","pdf":"","k":["Ignorance","Psychology","Epistemology","Philosophy"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00053-8","t":"Dynamisme et topoï argumentatifs","a":["Steffen Nordahl Lund"],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00053-8","pdf":"","k":["Argumentative","Argumentation theory","Meaning (existential)","Linguistics","Epistemology","Computer science","Sociology","Philosophy"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)90031-5","t":"IPrA news service","a":[],"d":"1998-11-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)90031-5","pdf":"","k":["Service (business)","Business","Marketing"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00009-5","t":"Persuasion and context: The pragmatics of academic metadiscourse","a":["Ken Hyland"],"d":"1998-10-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00009-5","pdf":"","k":["Metadiscourse","Rhetorical question","Persuasion","Pragmatics","Discipline","Context (archaeology)","Linguistics","Psychology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00037-x","t":"Language and masculinity","a":["Bettina Baron"],"d":"1998-10-01","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0378-2166(98)00037-x","pdf":"","k":["Masculinity","Psychology","Linguistics","Sociology","P